4. Simulate a Zomato-style scenario: Imagine you are a security tester for a food delivery app. Use Nmap to check if the test server at 'scanme.nmap.org' has any open ports that could be vulnerable. List any ports found and suggest what kind of services might be running on them.<br><br><em><strong>Hint:</strong> Use the command 'nmap scanme.nmap.org' and research the common services for the ports you find.</em>
=>Port	Service	What it typically means
22/tcp	SSH	Remote administrative login — if weakly configured (password auth, default creds, outdated version), it's a classic entry point for brute-force or credential-based attacks.
80/tcp	HTTP	Web server (historically Apache on Ubuntu/CentOS). Unencrypted web traffic — vulnerable to things like outdated CMS exploits, directory listing exposure, or (as shown in older scan logs) reflected/CSRF issues on contact forms.
443/tcp	HTTPS	Encrypted web server — same underlying app as port 80, just over TLS. Worth checking for outdated TLS versions/cipher suites

5. Refactor your Nmap scan command to only show open ports and their services, hiding all closed ports from the output.<br><br><em><strong>Constraint:</strong> Use the '-open' or '-sV' flag in your command to filter results.</em>
=>nmap -open -sV scanme.nmap.org

What each flag does:

-open tells Nmap to only display ports found in the open state in the output, suppressing the long "999 closed ports" filler line entirely.
-sV adds version detection, so instead of just "80/tcp open http," you get something like 80/tcp open http Apache httpd 2.4.7 (Ubuntu) — the actual service banner, which is what lets you cross-reference it against known CVEs.

Expected style of output (format, not fabricated live data):

PORT    STATE SERVICE VERSION
22/tcp  open  ssh     OpenSSH 6.6.1p1 Ubuntu
80/tcp  open  http    Apache httpd 2.4.7 (Ubuntu)

A good note for your report: pairing -open with -sV is a genuinely common real-world recon pattern — it cuts noise while giving you the exact software/version string you'd then check against a CVE database (like cve.mitre.org or nvd.nist.gov) for known exploits, rather than just knowing a port number in isolation.
